if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444592 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0be188f9-6651-53a5-a98d-3c5cc7f7f4e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444592 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for the integration by parts, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444593 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444593 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76452" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444593 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444593 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,0],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\pi ,0].$ Find $⟨t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444593 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37beaef3-36bd-59a9-8b05-4fe440be106e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444593 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for the integration by parts, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi }^{0}-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi }^{0}+{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+(-\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi ))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{0} \\\\  & =\\pi +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444594 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444594 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444594 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444594 #edb/ref 1071 "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is given by $⟨x,y⟩={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ Find $⟨x,y⟩$, if\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444594 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6652b923-fbed-582d-9443-3f7fe8880fb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444594 #edb/ref 1074 "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-3-3+2 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444595 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444595 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444595 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444595 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(3)y(3)+x(2)y(2)+x(1)y(1).\n$$\n\n Find $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$, if\n\n$$\nx(t)=1-2t+3{t}^{2},\\,y(t)=1-3{t}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444595 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ee8b63d-a039-5fa4-b12d-b0261d5f2b87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444595 #edb/ref 1074 "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =x(3)y(3)+x(2)y(2)+x(1)y(1) \\\\  & =22\\cdot (-26)+9\\cdot (-11)+2\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-572-99-4 \\\\  & =-675.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444596 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444596 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76439" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444596 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444596 #edb/ref 1071 "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is given by $⟨x,y⟩=3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ Find $⟨x,y⟩$, if\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444596 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "81ac8592-2b70-55bf-8038-041515b727a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444596 #edb/ref 1074 "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6-5+9 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444597 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444597 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43809" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444597 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444597 #edb/ref 1071 "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is given by $⟨x,y⟩=4{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ Find $⟨x,y⟩$, if\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444597 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "82c33805-9baa-5776-8533-64d6545bc8d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444597 #edb/ref 1074 "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =4{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+3{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+2{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 0\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =0-12+0 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444598 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444598 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140317" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444598 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444598 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V=C[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].$ Find $⟨5{cos}^{4}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444598 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f6d0c17-94d0-5de9-adca-7a206888f584" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444598 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨5{cos}^{4}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}5{cos}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}5{cos}^{4}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =-5{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}{cos}^{4}⁡t\\,(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-5\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{5}}{5}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-(1)^{5}+(0)^{5} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444599 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444599 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76443" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444599 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444599 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-1)y(-1)+x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1).\n$$\n\n Find $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$, if\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2}-4,\\,y(t)=3-t.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444599 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1906542-1837-55c7-bfa3-d76ad056eb3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444599 #edb/ref 1074 "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =x(-1)y(-1)+x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1) \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-12-12-6 \\\\  & =-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
